    "chunk_size_bytes": 1729,
    "chunk_text": "Takes from Overseas\n\nSurprise from staid BBC. Its director general, Tim Davie and chief executive of BBC News, Deborah Turness, resigned their posts on November 9. And as is usually the case now, in the eye of the storm is Prez Donald Trump. Not far behind is his Press Secretary, the very youthful, attractive and as we say \u2018too much\u2019 Karoline Leavitt \u2013 American political spokesperson who is the 36th White House Press Secretary, appointed by the person she so admires and makes no bones about. She has had many a verbal battle and opposition but continues in her post as said that she openly and reportedly admires Trump and adulation is what he seeks.\n\nThere was much written in the foreign press and announced over BBC News. I intend quoting Stephen Castle reporting from London in the NY Times of November 9.\n\n\u201cTwo of the top executives of the BBC resigned abruptly on Sunday following a report suggesting the public service broadcaster had misleadingly edited a speech by President Trump that preceded the January 6, 2021, Capitol riot.\u201d\n\nThis was a couple of days after The Daily Telegraph published details of a leaked internal memo arguing that a BBC Panorama documentary had juxtaposed comments by Mr Trump in a way that made it appear that he had explicitly encouraged the attack on Jan 6, 2021 Mr Davie admitted the BBC was not a perfect organization and had to be open, transparent and accountable. \u201cOverall the BBC has been delivering well but the current debate around BBC News has understandably contributed to my decision. As director general I have to take ultimate responsibility.\u201d Ms Turness echoed this by admitting she had to take responsibility for any mistakes made down the line below her."
