    "chunk_text": "During the 2021 assault on the Capitol her room was targeted and much destruction caused. In October, for political reasons, Paul Pelosi was savagely attacked and seriously injured. She was by his side for months during his recovery and convalescence. He is now OK.\n\nAlex Marshall reporting from the Booker Prize ceremony in London on November 11, in the NY Times, writes that when David Szalay\u2019s short listed novel All that Man Is was judged in 2016, sitting through the award ceremony was a horrible experience for the author and the end was worse since he lost to Paul Beatty\u2019s The Sellout. This year sitting through the ceremony was entirely different for him and his wife. His 2025 book, the sixth novel he has written, won the prestigious literary award. Author Roddy Doyle who chaired this year\u2019s judging panel noted the novel Flesh was singular and extraordinary.\n\nDavid Szalay, 51, Hungarian British author whose name is pronounced as Sol-lay, wrote previously about a sales executive who ends up stocking shelves in a grocery store; men in various states of duress; and the condition of man in modern society. Flesh beat novels by Susan Choi \u2013  Flashlight, Kiran Desai\u2019s The Loneliness of Sonia and Sunny, and three others.\n\nKiran Desai won the Booker in 2006 for her second novel The Inheritance of Loss at 35 years, while her mother had been short listed more than once for the prize. Her 2025 novel The Loneliness\u2026. takes place between 1996 and 2002 and is about an Indian immigrant couple who meet on a train and progress to falling in love and getting married. It is described as \u201can epic of love and family spanning generations and countries and most ambitious and accomplished work by Desai.\u201d She wrote after a long spell of hibernation from writing.\n\nMemories of my time at the Bandaranaike Centre for International Studies\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are 