    "md5": "a048b36f8fc6a85273697f79b99397cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "Germany seen as strengthening SL\u2019s disease preparedness\n\nGermany reaffirmed its continued commitment to Sri Lanka\u2019s health security and research collaboration through the renewal of funding under its Global Health Protection Programme (GHPP) \u2014 a move seen as strengthening both the country\u2019s disease preparedness and its broader economic resilience.\n\nSpeaking at the inauguration of the IDEANet One Health Forum 2025 at Colombo University on Monday, German ambassador to Sri Lanka and Maldives, Dr. Felix Neumann, highlighted that the partnership between the University of Colombo and Germany\u2019s Robert Koch Institute (RKI) stands as a model for science-driven development.\n\n\u201cThis partnership is a vital investment in health security \u2014 and health security is, in essence, economic security, Ambassador Neumann said. \u201cBy building laboratory and diagnostic capacity, Sri Lanka is not only improving its preparedness for emerging diseases but also strengthening the foundations for sustainable growth and human capital development.\u201d\n\nThe IDEANet collaboration, funded by Germany\u2019s Federal Ministry of Health, has already trained more than 200 Sri Lankan laboratory professionals, established nationwide PCR diagnostic networks and enabled the first-ever detection of Nipah virus in Sri Lanka.\n\n\u201cResearch collaboration accelerates innovation, drives knowledge transfer and creates long-term economic value, Dr. Neumann noted, adding that regular exchanges between German and Sri Lankan doctoral students have helped expand expertise and build future-ready talent. \u201cThis project exemplifies how international partnerships can deliver both scientific and socio-economic dividends.\u201d\n\nVice Chancellor of the University of Colombo, Professor Indika Mahesh Karunathilake, described the initiative as a \u201chistoric collaboration\u201d that demonstrates the value of interdisciplinary science and institutional partnerships."
  },
