    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c93b98675c885e21e027d2e7577a9a66",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "National statistics show a split personality in Sri Lanka\u2019s digital profile. On the one hand, broad digital literacy, the ability to use mobile phones and basic online services, is relatively high, roughly two out of three Sri Lankans demonstrate some form of digital literacy. On the other, formal computer literacy (the set of skills associated with using a personal computer: typing, file management, word processing, spreadsheets and basic software use) remains low. The Department of Census and Statistics reported an overall computer-literacy rate of 35.9% in 2024, down 3.1 percentage points from 2023, and with marked urban-rural disparities (urban 47.3%, rural and estate areas significantly lower). That gap matters because many mid-level and higher-value jobs still require computer competence rather than only smartphone fluency.\n\nThe 2026 estimates earmark funding to upgrade technical and vocational training (TVET) and to support education modernisation. Published budget pages and press coverage indicate about Rs. 8,000 million set aside for improving vocational training with specific reference to modernising courses and teacher training that includes technology components.\n\nThe Budget speech and independent analyses mention a new Digital Economy Act, the creation of a Digital Economy Authority, and measures to incentivise digital payments (for example waiving some transaction fees) to accelerate adoption of e-services. These legal and regulatory steps aim to create the governance structures to sustain investment and private-sector partnerships.\n\nTaken together, the Budget signals intent to both build infrastructure and to stimulate demand for digital services, a necessary combination if investments are to generate jobs and create real incentives for citizens to improve their digital skills. If implemented with discipline and equity in mind, the Budget\u2019s digital thrust could deliver concrete benefits:"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-11-17-islandlk-45143cc3",
    "num": "islandlk-45143cc3",
    "date_str": "2025-11-17",
    "description": "Where we stand, how Budget 2026 funds progress and what next",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/where-we-stand-how-budget-2026-funds-progress-and-what-next/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1763403360.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-11-17-islandlk-45143cc3-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d8912cdf6c4a2b3ba36f5b11a3e865bd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1705,
    "chunk_text": "Taken together, the Budget signals intent to both build infrastructure and to stimulate demand for digital services, a necessary combination if investments are to generate jobs and create real incentives for citizens to improve their digital skills. If implemented with discipline and equity in mind, the Budget\u2019s digital thrust could deliver concrete benefits:\n\nImproved job pathways: Upgraded TVET programmes tied to industry needs can create entry points into IT support, digital content creation, data operations and customer-service roles that do not require advanced degrees. The dedicated vocational funding in the budget provides the fiscal base for such retooling.\n\nEconomic multiplier effects: A stronger digital economy can stimulate entrepreneurship, remote work and exports of digital services the government itself has flagged ambitions for a USD 15 billion digital economy by 2030, which would create sustained demand for digital workers.\n\nPublic-sector efficiency and transparency: Digital procurement, e-payments and online workflows can reduce leakages and create measurable gains in service delivery benefits that are politically visible and socially meaningful.\n\nHowever every promise carries pitfalls. The Budget\u2019s success depends on implementation choices that are less glamorous than speeches or headline numbers. Equity shortfall. Centralised projects can paradoxically widen gaps if procurement, roll-out and teacher training focus on better connected urban schools. The DCS data showing stark urban-rural differences in computer literacy underline this risk: without explicit ring-fenced funding for remote and estate schools, the poorest children may be left further behind."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-11-17-islandlk-45143cc3",
    "num": "islandlk-45143cc3",
    "date_str": "2025-11-17",
    "description": "Where we stand, how Budget 2026 funds progress and what next",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/where-we-stand-how-budget-2026-funds-progress-and-what-next/",
