    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "aaa77633676358fa0ce0c118aa7bf51d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1479,
    "chunk_text": "Dedicate a visible tranche of the digital allocation to rural/estate connectivity, community learning centres and device maintenance. These ring-fenced sums should be conditional on provincial implementation plans and monitored centrally.\n\nCo-design short, stackable credentials with industry (BPOs, local IT firms, telecoms) so learners earn immediately employable skills; use apprenticeships and internship stipends to smooth the school-work transition.\n\nPublish procurement timelines, contracts and progress dashboards. Use third-party verification for large projects and empower Parliament\u2019s oversight committees and civil society to scrutinise roll-outs.\n\nStart with learners\u2019 existing digital behaviours (mobile apps, messaging) and scaffold keyboarding, file management, spreadsheets and basic coding. Micro-credentials should certify these steps and be portable across jobs and education pathways.\n\nIf the Budget is implemented with these principles, success will be visible in three ways within 03 -05 years: (a) measurable rises in computer literacy across provinces and narrowing of the urban-rural gap; (b) stronger TVET pipelines placing youth into digital economy jobs; and (c) more routine public services transacted online with lower administrative costs. These outcomes together would mean the government not only digitised services but also upgraded human capital, giving a generation of Sri Lankans the practical skills to participate in a digital economy."
