    "md5": "ed26b07af4182f9dbc29d2691469e228",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2013,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThis was my first role with such emotional depth and intensity, and I was excited to challenge myself.\n\n\u201cI took the role seriously, studying how a mother might feel and react in such an unimaginable situation.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe emotions felt very real while filming, and I remained in that feeling even after the Director said \u201ccut.\u201d\n\n\u201cThat moment taught me a lot about human pain and a mother\u2019s unconditional love.\u201d\n\nNoshin then went on to explain the story connected with \u2018Rayak Ho Payak\u2019 which is a collaboration between India and Sri Lanka (with both Indian and Sri Lankan actors), and this is the storyline:\n\nA young family from Chennai, in India, moves to Colombo, after the husband receives a new executive job, and soon after arriving in Sri Lanka, their daughter is kidnapped.\n\nThe film explores the emotional journey of a mother and father trying to find their child and highlights how life can change in an instant.\n\nFilmed in Chennai, and various locations in Sri Lanka, \u2018Rayak Ho Payak\u2019 shows the strength of a mother\u2019s love and the emotional resilience of a family facing tragedy.\n\nIn the US, Noshin worked in law and finance, and, most recently, as a Director of Finance.\n\n\u201cEducation has always been my foundation. My mother taught me the importance of independence and knowledge, and acting and storytelling have always been my passion, since childhood.\n\n\u201cI was active in dancing, modelling, commercials, singing, and school plays, and, after establishing a stable career, I decided to pursue acting full-time.\u201d\n\n\u201cThis is my first film in Sri Lanka, but language has not been a barrier. I speak Sinhala, English, Japanese, and a bit of Spanish.\n\n\u201cWhile I sometimes struggle with very formal Sinhala (like news broadcasts), I\u2019m continuously learning.\n\nWell, what\u2019s next for this extremely talented lady? And she went on to say that cinema is a universal language and that she loves Sri Lankan cinema,\n\n\u201cI\u2019m open to not limiting myself to one country or industry."
  },
