    "chunk_text": "SL\u2019s carbon market emerging as a \u2018flagship investment frontier\u2019\n\nSri Lanka is positioning its emerging carbon market as a flagship investment frontier capable of attracting hundreds of millions of dollars in new financing, senior officials said yesterday, unveiling a series of reforms aimed at aligning environmental governance with economic recovery.\n\nEnvironment Minister Dr. Dhammika Patabendi said global demand for high-quality carbon credits is rising sharply, creating an opportunity for Sri Lanka to monetise its natural assets responsibly.\n\nHe added: \u201cCarbon markets are a billion-dollar space and Sri Lanka cannot afford to stay on the sidelines. With credible monitoring and scientific baselines, we can turn forests, wetlands and renewable energy projects into sustainable financial assets.\u201d\n\nThe Environment Ministry expects Sri Lanka\u2019s carbon market to generate USD 250\u2013300 million annually within a decade. Officials said more than USD 500 million in climate-finance investment could flow into reforestation, mangroves, renewable energy and industrial decarbonisation projects over the first five years.\n\nSenior Adviser to the Ministry, Dr. Ravindra Kariyawasam, said the ministry\u2019s focus is on credibility and transparency.\n\n\u201cWe are building a science-driven, internationally aligned carbon-market architecture. Investors will see clear baselines, clear reporting standards and a system free from manipulation. That is the only way a market like this can work, Dr. Kariyawasam told The Island Financial Review.\n\nHe added that Sri Lanka is finalising a national registry, verification guidelines and project methodologies to meet global standards.\n\n\u201cOur aim is simple: every credit issued by Sri Lanka must be trusted and traceable. Once that trust is established, investment naturally follows, he said."
