    "md5": "95b93ec8a9a5448d4ebff0340c460fe3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "India\u2019s flagship rural roads programme (Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana \u2013 PMGSY) demonstrates how systematic rural connectivity transforms access to markets, schools, and health services while raising rural incomes. Large investments in all-weather roads reduced travel times and opened new livelihood opportunities across hundreds of thousands of habitations. For Sri Lanka, prioritized rural road networks and feeder routes to market hubs would immediately reduce post-harvest losses and transport costs.\n\nSri Lanka could pilot community-led rural renewal funds using a Saemaul-inspired approach, where village committees receive matched grants accompanied by technical assistance and measurable outcomes. Scaling digital inclusion through expanded fibre and mobile coverage for rural Divisional Secretariat, digital market platforms, digital payments, and tele-extension services can empower rural firms and farmers.\n\nRural MSMEs\u2014especially in post-conflict and COVID-affected districts\u2014require targeted refinancing, concessional loans, debt-restructuring mechanisms, regional SME venture funds, and diaspora investment windows. Modernizing value chains with investments in agro-processing centres, cold chains, certification facilities, and export facilitation\u2014drawing lessons from Vietnam\u2019s successful integration of producers into global markets\u2014can significantly increase rural incomes. Streamlined institutional coordination through a single Regional Industrial & Rural Development Authority would accelerate approvals, investor services, and land management. Strengthening human capital through vocational training in agro-processing, seafood handling, ICT, entrepreneurship, and expanding women\u2019s self-help or cooperative models will ensure inclusive, sustainable, and region-wide rural economic transformation."
  },
