    "md5": "69d13fcbfa92b97016e211ca6261eee6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "Moreover, rural transformation mitigates structural inequalities by creating employment opportunities outside urban centres. Investments in roads, irrigation, digital connectivity, and healthcare enhance quality of life and attract private-sector participation. As rural livelihoods become more resilient and stable, food security improves, and pressures on urban migration decline. In essence, rural development fosters a more balanced, equitable, and competitive national economy.\n\nSimultaneously, modern healthcare is indispensable for building a productive workforce and sustaining long-term economic development. Investments in hospitals, digital health systems, preventive care, and rural medical services reduce disease burdens and strengthen human capital. A healthier population participates more actively in the labour market, enjoys a better quality of life, and contributes to inclusive development outcomes.\n\nTogether, these three pillars form a virtuous cycle: rural communities become more connected, digital technologies expand economic opportunities, and advanced healthcare systems strengthen human capacity. The combined effect is a more equitable, resilient, and prosperous nation.\n\nThe development experiences of countries such as India, South Korea, Vietnam, and Singapore demonstrate that strategic investments in rural development, digital infrastructure, and healthcare are proven pathways to sustained national prosperity. Sri Lanka\u2019s geography, demographic potential, and skilled human capital position it to follow similar high-growth models.\n\nThe 2026 Budget reaffirms the government\u2019s commitment to improving living standards. It allocates Rs. 169 billion to ensure a decent and comfortable life for all citizens, Rs. 85.7 billion to expand access to safe drinking water, Rs. 13.5 billion for youth and sports initiatives, Rs. 19.6 billion for rural development, and Rs. 5.4 billion for urban infrastructure\u2014promoting balanced national growth."
  },
