    "chunk_size_bytes": 1677,
    "chunk_text": "Stifling democracy\n\nAll the signs are that there will be no Provincial Council (PC) elections next year. The government\u2019s decision to appoint a Parliamentary Select Committee (PSC) to inquire into matters pertaining to the delayed PC polls is an indication that the ruling NPP is afraid of facing an election any time soon and therefore all out to prevent its popularity and electoral strength from being put to test. It is using the same modus operandi as the previous governments to postpone the PC polls further\u2014the delimitation of electoral boundaries. It has demonstrated that it has no sense of shame, just like those who made the PC polls disappear, as it were, eight years ago.\n\nOne may recall that electorates were delimited during the UNP-led Yahapalana government as a legal requirement for the implementation of the new mixed proportional system, but the report of the Delimitation Committee was not approved by Parliament. In August 2018, when a division was taken on the report following a debate, 139 MPs voted against it. There wasn\u2019t a single vote for the report, which was required to be approved by a two-thirds majority, for the PC polls to be held under the new electoral system.\n\nThe Election Commission (EC) has reportedly said a fresh delimitation process will take about one year to reach completion. A PSC can be made to drag on, delaying the PC polls indefinitely. This seems to be the NPP\u2019s battle plan. It is said that when a government fears the people, there is liberty. But when a government fears elections, democracy is in jeopardy. Since 2017, three governments have kept the PC polls in limbo by refusing to take action to hold them."
  },
