    "md5": "e42050dae74669fa03674d2841c38595",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1795,
    "chunk_text": "More serious attachments were sometimes forged with hostel mates. There was the desire to be close to a friend, sleep with her if possible and some sort of special love grew. A bit of fondling would also arise. But this was all a passing phase and not to be cautious about. Once in a while it was serious and one or both partners would develop homosexual preferences; boys were irrelevant to them, even disliked.\n\nIt was the time of the LTTE so an oft acted theme was children having no bias but parents adversely influencing them. In the poorer schools travails of a home with a drunken father was often presented. The point that hit me forcefully was that coed schools had boys and girls mixing and acting together very comfortably. No barriers at all to easy camaraderie.\n\nNot so in a Muslim school I went to. Principal objection was the getting together of boys and girls from the two branch schools. Once I insisted, I found the air in the hall we were in, palpable with sexuality. The way the boys and girls exchanged looks was, if I may mention so, disgusting. I realized it was due to all the gender taboos these students were subject to.\n\nTo come to the point of this article which is my opinion on sex education in schools. As a writer of a letter to the editor recently opined it is more urgent and relevant to impart information on drug abuse and prevention to school kids. Drug use is rampant in schools and getting graver. Knowing about dangerous drugs is much more important and urgent than deviant sex. But I say sex education is important and necessary: what evils prevail; how a child can be aware of sex predators and avoid them; how to act if approached and what help is available. The two issues can be included in the school syllabus but probably for Grade seven and above."
