    "chunk_size_bytes": 1703,
    "chunk_text": "The launch of The Magic Silver Tree and Puduma Ridi Gaha and the exhibition, celebrating Sybil\u2019s unique style of storytelling and illustrations, are open to the public from 24th to 25th November, 2025.\n\nSybil Wettasinghe (October 31, 1928 \u2013 June 30, 2020) was a pioneering figure in Sri Lankan children\u2019s literature, whose work continues to inspire generations.\n\nHer career as a writer began at Lake House Newspapers, through the encouragement of Dharmapala Wettasinghe, then Chief Subeditor of Janatha newspaper, who later became her husband. When the regular story writer was absent one day, he invited Sybil to contribute a story\u2014Kuda Hora (The Umbrella Thief), her first story became an instant favorite among young readers and marked the beginning of a remarkable career in storytelling and illustration.\n\nKuda Hora, originally illustrated in black and white, was published in 1956 as a Navamaga supplementary reader and has remained in continuous publication for over 65 years. In 1983, Sybil re-illustrated the book in color and entered it into Japan\u2019s prestigious Noma Concours Picture Book Illustrations Competition, where she won third place. This recognition led to  Kuda Hora being translated and published in Japan, and later in Korea, China, Sweden, and Denmark.\n\nIn total, Sybil Wettasinghe authored and illustrated over 400 children\u2019s books, in addition to illustrating hundreds more for other authors. Eight of her books were published in Japan, the last being Magic Silver Tree. She wrote in both Sinhala and English, with many of her work being translated into Tamil.\n\nSybil passed away peacefully on June 30, 2020, leaving behind an enduring literary and artistic legacy."
