    "md5": "b3307f356198011134ed2e93794e98dc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka adds two more Bat Species to Its Mammal Record\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s bat researchers have confirmed the presence of two free-tailed bat species never before recorded in the country, marking a significant expansion of the island\u2019s known mammal diversity.\n\nThe breakthrough documented by Dr. Tharaka Kusuminda and his team; Amani Mannakkara, Bruce Patterson, Chamara Amarasinghe and Professor Wipula Bandara Yapa, adds both Wroughton\u2019s free-tailed bat (Otomops wroughtoni) and the East Asian free-tailed bat (Tadarida insignis) to Sri Lanka\u2019s bat fauna. The findings were published following extensive acoustic surveys conducted across the central highlands and tea-estate landscapes.\n\nSpeaking to The Island, Dr. Tharaka Kusuminda, lead author of the study, said the discovery reshapes what scientists know about the island\u2019s molossid bats.\n\n\u201cThese two free-tailed bats were long suspected to occur in Sri Lanka, but until now we lacked verified evidence,\u201d Dr. Kusuminda explained.\n\n\u201cOur acoustic data clearly match the species-level characteristics documented in India and Nepal, leaving no doubt that both species are part of Sri Lanka\u2019s bat community.\u201d\n\nThe team captured nine echolocation call series of Otomops wroughtoni and 19 of Tadarida insignis from tea plantations in Kotagala, Gammaduwa, Kotapola, Balangoda and Nuwara Eliya.\n\nDr. Kusuminda noted that the calls were unmistakable:\u201dThe peak frequencies, call structures and pattern sequences all aligned perfectly with published data from India and the Himalayan region. These species are extremely fast fliers, difficult to observe visually, so acoustic identification was the only reliable method.\u201d\n\nThe discovery of Otomops wroughtoni in Sri Lanka is particularly remarkable. Classified by the IUCN as Near Threatened, the species is known for its rarity and limited distribution."
