    "md5": "dba4b3de58189e0700269f33af4f780d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "Dr. Kusuminda noted that the calls were unmistakable:\u201dThe peak frequencies, call structures and pattern sequences all aligned perfectly with published data from India and the Himalayan region. These species are extremely fast fliers, difficult to observe visually, so acoustic identification was the only reliable method.\u201d\n\nThe discovery of Otomops wroughtoni in Sri Lanka is particularly remarkable. Classified by the IUCN as Near Threatened, the species is known for its rarity and limited distribution.\n\n\u201cThe presence of Wroughton\u2019s free-tailed bat suggests strong biogeographical connectivity with the Western Ghats,\u201d Dr. Kusuminda said.\n\n\u201cIts low-frequency echolocation calls, around 16 kHz on average, were distinct and consistent across all localities.\u201d\n\nThe study also recorded the East Asian free-tailed bat, Tadarida insignis, with an even lower mean peak frequency of 12.51 kHz. The bat\u2019s extremely shallow FM (frequency-modulated) calls were well differentiated from other South Asian molossid species. Dr. Kusuminda said the species may have been overlooked for decades:\n\n\u201cThe call structure of Tadarida insignis is very similar to the calls of other molossids, but when analysed carefully, the frequency drop and bandwidth make clear distinctions. Without acoustic tools, detecting this species would have been nearly impossible.\u201d\n\nLocal mammalogists say the discoveries highlight the importance of sustained acoustic monitoring.\n\nSenior bat researcher Prof. W. B. Yapa, who has extensively documented Sri Lankan mammals, welcomed the new additions:\n\n\u201cSri Lanka\u2019s bat diversity is richer than often assumed. Acoustic surveys, combined with advanced statistical methods like PCA, are now proving essential to uncover hidden biodiversity.\u201d\n\nInternational experts have also taken note. The study shows that call patterns in Sri Lankan individuals fall well within the ranges published in India and Nepal, suggesting low geographic variation in key call parameters."
