    "md5": "0e95aab7b96c28db0e8db397794de119",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1797,
    "chunk_text": "International experts have also taken note. The study shows that call patterns in Sri Lankan individuals fall well within the ranges published in India and Nepal, suggesting low geographic variation in key call parameters.\n\nDr. Kusuminda said these modified landscapes may offer ideal flying corridors:\n\n\u201cOpen spaces above tea cover allow free-tailed bats to maintain the high flight speeds they require. Our recordings show repeated use of these habitats, especially at dawn and dusk.\u201d\n\nTime-gap analyses between call series\u2014ranging from 1 minute 28 seconds to nearly 9 hours\u2014suggest the same individuals were not being repeatedly counted, strengthening the validity of the findings.\n\nSri Lanka now hosts four confirmed molossid bat species, and the findings call for increased conservation attention.\n\n\u201cSome of these species may be using small roosts, fissures or building structures that go unnoticed,\u201d Dr. Kusuminda cautioned.\n\n\u201cWith more survey effort, we may uncover even more cryptic species.\u201d\n\nHe said ongoing landscape development\u2014especially in highland regions\u2014must consider the needs of fast-flying aerial insectivores.\n\nThe discovery comes at a time when global bat populations face growing threats from habitat loss, climate change and disease. For Sri Lanka, two newly confirmed species add weight to the island\u2019s position as a biodiversity hotspot.\n\nDr. Kusuminda concluded: \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s bat fauna continues to surprise us. Our work shows that with the right tools, we can reveal species that have been here for decades, undetected. This is only the beginning.\u201d\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
