    "description": "American words gaining currency in English",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/american-words-gaining-currency-in-english/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1763904060.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-11-23-islandlk-c36f09fb-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bf322912511c68f91e225d06fc438bb2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1975,
    "chunk_text": "There are some grammatical differences between British English and American English. There are a number of nouns that are used in the singular in British English but in the plural in American English.\n\nIn both British and American English the preposition \u2018on\u2019 is used when naming the day when something happens: The Bribery Commission said on Monday that it would file a case against the former minister. In American English the preposition \u2018on\u2019 is usually omitted: President Trump said Monday he would launch an independent investigation into the matter. In British English the word \u2018home\u2019 is used when the idea is that of being there. The word \u2018home\u2019 on its own is used when the idea is of going there: She stayed at home. In American English \u2018home\u2019 can be used without the preposition \u2018at\u2019: I am going to stay home tonight.\n\nPunctuation is another area where the differences between British English and American English are prominent. In British English the dot at the end of a sentence is called \u2018full stop.\u2019 In American English it is called \u2018period.\u2019 In American English full stops are used in most abbreviations: Mr., Dr. and Mrs. However, such full stops are not used in British English: Mr, Dr and Mrs. In British English the word following a colon starts with a simple letter whereas in American English a colon is followed by a capital letter.\n\nIn British English the dates are written in figures. The order is usually day, month and year. In American English the order is usually month, day and year. The use of the hash mark in British English and pound sign in American English means \u2018number.\u2019 The hash mark is very popular among advertisers.\n\nDespite such differences, Americans read British books and newspapers without difficulty. Similarly, those who have studied British English can easily understand American books, magazines, films and songs. The barriers between British English and American English are breaking down."
