    "chunk_size_bytes": 1837,
    "chunk_text": "State workers, especially teachers and principals, played a very active role in bringing the NPP to power, last year, as evident from the massive swing in the postal votes for it. The government has kicked away the ladder and antagonised many trade unions. Kalutara District United Teachers\u2019 Union Secretary, Tharindu Dilshan, has said teachers now regret ever having campaigned and voted for the NPP! General Secretary of the Ceylon Teachers\u2019 Union Joseph Stalin has claimed that even the JVP\u2019s Ceylon Teachers Service Union, once led by Deputy Minister Mahinda Jayasinghe, has closed ranks with the protesting trade unions. The governments of self-proclaimed leftists are no different from the capitalist regimes in their handling of labour disputes; they, too, ride roughshod over trade unions after winning elections with the help of workers. One may recall how the United Front government, comprising leftists, especially the LSSP, broke a bank employees\u2019 strike in the early 1970s.\n\nPrime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya, who is also the Minister of Education, was in the vanguard of a university teachers\u2019 protest campaign to pressure the Rajapaksa government to allocate funds amounting to 6% of GDP for education, in 2012, when she was in the groves of Academe. PM Amarasuriya, her Cabinet colleagues and President Anura Kumara Dissanayake brag that the state coffers are overflowing. Most of all, the President has decided to procure as many as 1,750 brand new double-cab pickup trucks for the MPs and state officials. The government has decided to absorb 50% of the cost of estate workers\u2019 wage hikes, without passing it on to big plantation companies. So, the question is why the NPP\u2008government cannot allocate more funds for education and resolve the issues that have driven teachers and principals to protest."
