    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "LKY emphasised that stability and good governance\u2014not ideology\u2014drive development. He criticised political systems where media manipulation, image-building, and spin overshadow competence. He argued that leaders like Churchill or Roosevelt could not emerge from election environments dominated by packaging rather than substance.\n\nHe contrasted these failures with countries that permitted market forces to function, such as Kenya, which\u2014despite corruption\u2014achieved stronger economic outcomes.\n\nWorld War II profoundly shaped LKY\u2019s worldview. The collapse of British power, the cruelty of Japanese occupation, and the violent struggles that followed left deep impressions. These experiences convinced him that survival required self-reliance, national unity, and a realistic understanding of power.\n\nAfter Singapore\u2019s separation from Malaysia in 1965, LKY faced the urgent task of building a nation from scratch. The early years brought anxiety, not celebration.\n\nSingapore\u2019s survival depended on economic viability. LKY first attracted investors from Hong Kong and Taiwan, then from Europe, Japan, and the United States. China\u2019s Cultural Revolution became an unexpected opportunity: major technology firms such as Texas Instruments and Hewlett-Packard relocated operations to Singapore.\n\nSingapore offered what the region lacked: political stability, industrial peace, and English fluency. Within two decades Singapore became a global electronics hub.\n\nLKY\u2019s long-term strategy was to transform Singapore into a first-world environment that could attract global talent. World-class infrastructure\u2014airport, seaport, telecommunications\u2014was built, and public safety, health standards, and urban amenities were raised to international levels. Campaigns to improve courtesy, cleanliness, and civic behaviour reinforced the shift.\n\nThe \u201cGarden City\u201d initiative became one of Singapore\u2019s most successful image-building efforts."
