    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "LKY\u2019s long-term strategy was to transform Singapore into a first-world environment that could attract global talent. World-class infrastructure\u2014airport, seaport, telecommunications\u2014was built, and public safety, health standards, and urban amenities were raised to international levels. Campaigns to improve courtesy, cleanliness, and civic behaviour reinforced the shift.\n\nThe \u201cGarden City\u201d initiative became one of Singapore\u2019s most successful image-building efforts.\n\nMixed housing policies prevented racial enclaves. National service built a shared sense of duty. Assistance from Israel helped create a credible defence force, essential for deterrence and national confidence.\n\nLKY believed leaders must act as trustees of the nation, not beneficiaries of office. Clean government, strict accountability, and a politically neutral civil service were essential pillars. Corruption had to be defeated before it took root. Meritocracy was institutionalised through an independent Public Service Commission.\n\nTo bind citizens to the nation, LKY introduced mass home ownership through the Central Provident Fund. Today, more than 90% of Singaporean households own their homes. This sense of stakeholding strengthened national stability and social cohesion.\n\nLKY rejected fashionable ideologies of his day\u2014anti-Americanism, anti-multinationalism, and the belief that democracy is a prerequisite for development. He argued that honest, stable, effective government matters more than form.\n\nEducation was Singapore\u2019s foundation for advancement. English-medium instruction expanded rapidly, supported by technical institutes, polytechnics, and universities. This prepared Singaporeans for global industries and secured the nation\u2019s economic future."
