    "md5": "68dd3e0cebde086905a3a00c2f854b60",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Education was Singapore\u2019s foundation for advancement. English-medium instruction expanded rapidly, supported by technical institutes, polytechnics, and universities. This prepared Singaporeans for global industries and secured the nation\u2019s economic future.\n\nBy the early 1990s, Singapore had a new generation of leaders capable of maintaining integrity and high standards.\n\nSingapore\u2019s judiciary became one of the most respected in Asia. Strong courts and predictable rules created public confidence and attracted investment.\n\nLKY contrasted the crisis-driven leadership of his generation with the opinion-poll-driven politics of mature democracies. When Singapore faced existential threats in the 1960s, decisions had to be made quickly. Disorder, strikes, and racial tension could have destroyed the nation.\n\nIntegration in housing and schools was implemented decisively\u2014not through endless debate\u2014because the long-term national interest was clear.\n\nGovernments must use the mandate given by voters to exercise judgment, not govern by daily plebiscite. The revolutionary generation had trust and unity; later generations, raised in comfort, expect debate and consultation\u2014both necessary but potentially paralysing if taken to excess.\n\nLKY believed both innate qualities and historical necessity shape leaders. Crises produce statesmen; comfort produces managers. The examples of Mao and Deng, Churchill and Attlee, Reagan and Carter illustrate how circumstances shape leadership types.\n\nLKY acknowledged his earlier support for absolute press freedom, but argued that in Singapore\u2019s vulnerable early years, open debate on sensitive issues could have led to riots. Pragmatism, not ideology, guided his media policy.\n\n= We are a multiracial, multireligious society with far greater natural resources than Singapore. Unity is our missing ingredient.\n\n= English should serve as the working language for national integration and global competitiveness, while preserving mother tongues and faiths."
