    "md5": "18ccf0fe727f10b9529267cd6f2533b6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1610,
    "chunk_text": "A bid to hose down the Augean stables\n\nA pilot project has been launched to enable passengers to use bank cards to pay bus fares on some long-distance routes. This is a long-felt need, and it is hoped that the new scheme will be expanded to cover all bus routes gradually. Teething problems are bound to crop up as the new system gets underway, and they must not be allowed to stand in the way of the continuation of digital payment of bus fares.\n\nPresident of the Lanka Bus Owners\u2019 Association Gemunu Wijeratne has been at the forefront of a campaign to have a digital fare payment system introduced by successive governments, albeit in vain. Curiously, he has expressed doubts about the success of the pilot project currently underway. He knows bus conductors for what they really are more than anyone else does, and his argument that it is ill-advised to allow conductors to handle the card-reading machines is not without merit. Ideally, there should be no conductors at all on buses, but the public transport sector is so chaotic that it is not possible to collect fares in crowded buses without them. So, the success of the new fare payment system will largely hinge on the elimination of the factors that have made bus conductors indispensable.\n\nWijeratne\u2019s concerns are not unfounded. Sri Lankans\u2019 brains are hardwired to work around systems. Bus conductors know more than one way to shoe a horse, and they will go to great lengths to rig the digital payment system or make it fail. Vigilance, public cooperation and strict regulation are essential for the success of the new fare payment system."
  },
