    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1e5de14b6b04c9ab5678e9b8feaf1508",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Wijeratne\u2019s concerns are not unfounded. Sri Lankans\u2019 brains are hardwired to work around systems. Bus conductors know more than one way to shoe a horse, and they will go to great lengths to rig the digital payment system or make it fail. Vigilance, public cooperation and strict regulation are essential for the success of the new fare payment system.\n\nMost buses in Sri Lanka are not worthy of the name as former Cabinet Minister Champika Ranawaka has rightly pointed out; they are merely lorry chassis fitted with seats. Only buses exclusively designed for passenger transport allow orderly boarding and alighting and digital payment of fares, without conductors. The need for phasing out the existing jalopies and replacing them with modern passenger buses cannot be overstated.\n\nMeanwhile, the digital payment system is also expected to be an antidote to extortion; private bus operators complain that they have to pay extortionist rings daily, and the protection money accounts for a big chunk of their revenue. Thanks to their political connections, extortionists have been able to defeat some half-hearted attempts by successive governments to neutralise them. It is incumbent upon the JVP-led NPP government to end this menace. Former President of the Matara Private Bus Owners\u2019 Association Aruna Kalpage revealed yesterday that private buses could not ply between Matara and Colombo, without paying protection money about 20 times a day. The same is more or less true of the buses operating on other roads.\n\nExtortionists working for dangerous underworld figures operate openly in bus stations, as is public knowledge. These characters must be made to face the full force of the law. One can only hope that the NPP government will order a crackdown on these anti-social elements and liberate the private bus service from their clutches."
  },
