    "md5": "bb0b280d0f1aa41cbd37d61603db2de4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "Fourth solo trip\u2026 Down Under\n\nMelantha Perera, who made a big impact in the scene here, performing with the group Mirage, and several other top local bands, is now a sing-along star.\n\nHe is much in demand in Australia \u2013 as a solo artiste \u2013 and, last Sunday, left to oblige his fans in Perth (first time) and in Sydney and Melbourne.\n\nIn fact, this is his fourth solo trip \u2026 Down Under; he was last there in September, 2024.\n\nHe says he is looking forward to this particular trip, with much enthusiasm, as he will be involved with the band Redemption, in Sydney, and with Cameron Colombege\u2019s outfit in Melbourne.\n\nBoth venues will be sing-along/dance events, said Melantha, adding that in Perth he will be introducing his MelaNota music notation system, designed to make music learning more accessible, and will also get those present to sing-along with him.\n\n\u201cI\u2019m extremely grateful to Samantha Fernando, CEO of Business Certification System, Sydney, for making my fourth trip to Australia a reality.\u201d\n\nMelantha is the founder of Riversheen School of Music, where he teaches guitar, keyboard, vocals, and drums to students of all ages and skill levels.\n\nHe\u2019s also the trainer and mentor of Bright Light, Sri Lanka\u2019s first Western music pop outfit, comprising visually impaired individuals.\n\nUnder his guidance, Bright Light is doing exceptionally well \u2026 as a visually impaired outfit.\n\nThey recently released their second original song, which is an A Capella, called \u2018In The Absence of Light,\u2019 during the opening of Audiobooks at the MJ Centre, in Katubedde, Moratuwa.\n\nThe song could be checked out on YouTube (Riversheen School of Music).\n\nAudiobooks, at the MJ Centre, is a library for the visually impaired.\n\nMelantha Perera\u2019s contributions to Sri Lankan music are significant, and his work with Bright Light has been particularly inspiring, showcasing his commitment to music education and social impact."
