    "chunk_size_bytes": 1511,
    "chunk_text": "POIA\u2019s AGM drives a point: The case for revisiting Sri Lanka\u2019s oil palm ban\n\nThe Annual General Meeting of the Palm Oil Industry Association (POIA), held at Cinnamon Grand Colombo on Nov. 24, served as an insightful platform to challenge the prevailing narrative surrounding oil palm in Sri Lanka. The keynote address by Dr. Ranjith Mahindapala, a past president of the National Academy of Sciences, presented a compelling, evidence-based case to lift the industry\u2019s controversial ban.\n\nThe 2021 government ban was enacted in response to public dissatisfaction, fueled by claims that oil palm is an environmental villain \u2013 accused of guzzling water, degrading soil, and causing deforestation, Dr. Mahindapala recalled.\n\nHe argued that this decision was a reaction to vociferous, unsubstantiated voices rather than scientific fact.\n\nHe presented findings from an independent, comprehensive study by Solidaridad that systematically debunks these myths one by one. The allegation of excessive water use? The independent Solidaridad study reveals water consumption is around 300 litres per day, often met by ample rainfall in the Southern Province.\n\nThe fear of high soil erosion? Oil palm is not planted on slopes over 40%, as per official guidelines from the Coconut Research Institute.\n\nConcerns over fertiliser and chemicals? The nutrient need is about twice, not ten times, that of rubber, and pest issues are comparable to coconut.\n\nThe economic argument for oil palm is even more striking, he argued."
