    "chunk_text": "Concerns over fertiliser and chemicals? The nutrient need is about twice, not ten times, that of rubber, and pest issues are comparable to coconut.\n\nThe economic argument for oil palm is even more striking, he argued.\n\n\u201cThe existing policy, which mandates replacing oil palm with rubber, is counterproductive. It replaces a high-yield, economically superior crop with a less profitable one, ultimately weakening the rural economy,\u201d he noted.\n\nDr. Mahindapala\u2019s appeal was clear: Sri Lanka must transition to evidence-based policymaking. The ban should be lifted and replaced with science-led best practices to ensure environmental sustainability. He further proposed that policymakers visit Malaysia and Indonesia to witness firsthand that the local fears are largely unfounded.\n\nIn conclusion he said,\u201d It is time to see oil palm not as a threat, but as a key to agricultural prosperity and import substitution.\u201d\n\nConquering ups and downs share market ends on stable footing\n\nWNPS in fine balancing act with govt. to facilitate conservation action\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
