    "md5": "99ce972ec56cd6c753bb174c75b20c38",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1776,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018Big Brother\u2019 cult on the rise?\n\nThe Criminal Investigation Department (CID) has apparently prioritised the summoning of journalists over conducting serious investigations. It is apparently preoccupied with political work.\n\nEditor of the Aruna newspaper Mahinda Ileperuma has been summoned by the CID for questioning on a news story that one has to produce a letter from the Chairman of the Public Security Committee (PSC) in one\u2019s residential area, in addition to a Grama Niladari certificate, to obtain a police report. Health and Mass Media Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa and Public Security Minister Ananda Wijepala vehemently denied the news item, in Parliament. The JVP/NPP exercises control over the PSCs. Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa told Parliament that the media reports on the issue were accurate. But Minister Jayatissa inveighed against Illeperuma and delivered a long lecture on media ethics.\n\nOpposition Leader Premadasa yesterday gave the lie to the government\u2019s claim at issue once again. A notice put up at the Matara Police Station stated that letters from the PSC Chairpersons had to be attached to the applications for police reports, Premadasa told Parliament, tabling a copy of the relevant document. He also tabled the copies of letters issued by a PSC Chairman to the police, recommending the issuance of police reports to two individuals. So, the CID has nothing to probe; the media reports in question are factually correct.\n\nMost of all, there is nothing criminal about the newspaper reports on the PSCs and police clearance certificates. It therefore defies comprehension why the CID has been called in to conduct an investigation. The government is obviously trying to intimidate the media, control information and suppress dissent."
  },
